; ============================================================================
;  STREET  --  the outdoor half of the session: front garden, pavement, road
;  with a marked crossing, and the far pavement with a postbox to walk to.
;
;  WHY IT IS HERE. Falls outdoors are not mostly about the road surface, they
;  are about the approach: stepping down an unfamiliar kerb, being pushed off
;  the pavement by a bin, and crossing without looking. The house teaches
;  hazard recognition; the street teaches the walk.
;
;  LAYOUT (metres, y = 0 is the house floor; outside is one 140 mm step down)
;    z 1.36 ..  7.20   front garden, with a 1 m path from the front door
;    z 7.20 ..  9.20   near pavement, kerb at its far edge
;    z 9.30 .. 15.10   the road, one lane each way, crossing at x = 0
;    z 15.20 .. 17.20  far pavement, postbox and bench
;    z 17.40 ..        neighbouring houses, for context only
;
;  The crossing is DROPPED at x -1.8 .. 1.8 (no kerb there) and marked with
;  bars. Everything walkable -- path, pavements, road -- is in the "walkable"
;  group, so the teleport arc accepts it; grass and walls are not, which keeps
;  the participant on the route without a single invisible wall.
;
;  THE ROAD IS DELIBERATELY WALKABLE. A training tool that makes crossing badly
;  impossible cannot measure whether the participant would have. StreetCrossing
;  scores every step into the road instead, and says the one useful thing at the
;  moment it matters.
;
;  The car (StreetCar) travels at 3.5 m/s with an 11 s gap between runs, never
;  collides, and eases to a stop for anyone already in the road.
; ============================================================================

[node name="Street" type="Node3D"]

[node name="RoomZone" type="Area3D" parent="."]
